win32u, dwrite: Chinese, Japanese and Korean text with the Mac's own fonts

Windows programs name Windows' East Asian fonts ("MS Gothic", "Microsoft YaHei", "Malgun
Gothic", ...) and Wine's font links and DirectWrite's fallback name them or Linux's Noto CJK
fonts. A Mac has none of these, so in a FineWine prefix every font, Wine's own dialog fonts
included, drew Chinese, Japanese and Korean characters as empty boxes, through GDI and through
DirectWrite (Chromium-based launchers) alike. The code pages were never the problem: 932, 936,
949 and 950 convert correctly, and the ANSI code page follows the locale.

- wine.inf: HKCU\Software\Wine\Fonts\Replacements maps each Windows East Asian font name to the
  Mac font that stands in for it (Hiragino Sans and Hiragino Mincho for Japanese, Hiragino Sans
  GB, Songti SC and Heiti SC for Simplified Chinese, Heiti TC and Songti TC for Traditional,
  Apple SD Gothic Neo for Korean; PingFang and Kaiti first when the user has downloaded them).
  GDI and DirectWrite both read the key; a real font of the Windows name, if installed, wins.
  With those names present, Wine's existing font links resolve, so every font falls back to
  them for characters it does not have.
- win32u: when MS Shell Dlg is set to a font none of Wine's link lists is keyed on, the list is
  picked by the ANSI code page instead of none at all, and the System font takes Microsoft Sans
  Serif's links when Tahoma (substituted) has none. Windows orders its links by the system
  locale too; the order decides which language's forms shared Han characters take.
- dwrite: after each Noto CJK font in the system fallback comes the font Windows falls back to
  (Yu Gothic UI, Microsoft YaHei, Microsoft JhengHei, Malgun Gothic), which the replacements
  above make the Mac's fonts. A region locale ("zh-CN", "ko-KR") now finds the mappings made for
  its script or language ("zh-Hans", "ko"); only exact names matched before, so Chinese and
  Korean text took the locale-neutral (Japanese) forms.

Checked by tests/runtime/cjk.c in Endfield_FineWine (scripts/test-finewine-runtime.sh).

